    <h2>1 · About Sentence Piano</h2>
    <p>Sentence Piano is a free, offline web page — one file that opens in any browser on a tablet or a computer, with nothing to install and no internet needed. It presents a colourful on‑screen keyboard where the letter keys play musical notes. A child can play freely, or type a sentence and hear it played back as a tune.</p>
    <p>Every letter A–Z is mapped to a note from a single <strong>C‑major octave scale</strong> (the notes cycle C, D, E, F, G, A, B, C). Because everything stays in one key, <strong>whatever a child types always sounds pleasant</strong> — there are no “wrong” notes, which keeps the experience rewarding and low‑stress.</p>
    <h3>What it develops</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Fine motor skills</strong> — locating and pressing keys, isolating a finger, building from single presses to sequences.</li>
      <li><strong>Typing &amp; keyboard familiarity</strong> — finding letters on a real QWERTY layout, left‑to‑right order, the space bar.</li>
      <li><strong>Letter–sound &amp; phonics</strong> — matching a letter's shape, name and sound; hearing sounds in words.</li>
      <li><strong>Early writing</strong> — building words and simple sentences, word spaces, copying a modelled sentence.</li>
      <li><strong>Music</strong> — beat, rhythm, note length, pitch, tempo and dynamics; composing, performing and responding.</li>
      <li><strong>Communication &amp; social skills</strong> — turn‑taking, choice‑making, sharing and responding to a tune.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>The companion files</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sentence Piano</strong> — the main tool (Free play + Follow along).</li>
      <li><strong>Wall chart</strong> — a printable letter → note → colour chart to display beside the device.</li>
      <li><strong>Simple keyboard</strong> — a 7‑key version (A S D F G H J) for the very first cause‑and‑effect stage.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- 2 -->
    <h2>2 · Getting started</h2>
    <p>Open the tool by tapping or double‑clicking it. On some browsers the sound starts after the <strong>first tap or key press</strong> — this simply “wakes up” the audio and is completely normal.</p>
    <h3>The parts of the screen</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Free play mode</strong> — invent tunes from your own words; each key plays instantly as you type.</li>
      <li><strong>Follow along mode</strong> — copy a target sentence; the next letter glows, correct keys light up and tick off, and finishing a sentence earns a ⭐ and plays the tune back.</li>
      <li><strong>The on‑screen keyboard</strong> — tap the colour‑coded keys (great on a tablet); each colour is a note.</li>
      <li><strong>“Your sentence as music” strip</strong> — tap a coloured letter tile and pick its note length from the note pictures (quaver, crotchet, minim or semibreve); longer notes look wider.</li>
      <li><strong>▶ Play it back</strong> — plays the sentence in 4/4 time: each letter is one beat, each space is a one‑beat rest.</li>
      <li><strong>Speed &amp; Notes buttons</strong> — Slow / Medium / Fast tempo, and show or hide the note names.</li>
      <li><strong>⚙️ For grown‑ups</strong> — a discreet button where an adult can type a custom sentence for the child to copy.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>How the sound works (for planning)</h3>
    <p>Each letter plays its note; a space is a silent beat (a rest). Note lengths follow standard values — a quaver is half a beat, a crotchet one beat, a minim two beats and a semibreve four beats — and four beats make one bar of 4/4. This lets the same tool move from “press a key, hear a sound” all the way to composing and reading simple rhythm.</p>

    <!-- 3 -->
    <h2>3 · A note on the Victorian Curriculum (2026)</h2>
    <p>Victoria is implementing the <strong>Victorian Curriculum F–10 Version 2.0</strong>. English and Mathematics have been in place since 2025, and the Arts (including Music) from 2026.</p>
    <p><strong>Foundation Levels A–D</strong> (“Towards Foundation”) provide a developmentally sequenced continuum for students with intellectual or cognitive disability or learning delay, leading into the Foundation level. In 2026, English Foundation Levels A–D continue under Version 1.0. Throughout this guide, <strong>Level 2 is equivalent to Year 2.</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>English (Version 2.0):</strong> strands are <strong>Language, Literature and Literacy</strong>; achievement is reported across three modes — <strong>Speaking and Listening, Reading and Viewing, and Writing.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Music (Version 2.0):</strong> strands are <strong>Exploring, Developing Practices, Creating and Presenting</strong>. At Foundation the named musical elements include <strong>beat, rhythm, pitch, dynamics and tempo</strong>, and discriminating sound from silence, loud from soft, and fast from slow.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- 4 -->
